<Base
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="Privacy policy for DaoismHub — what data the sites do and do not process, legal bases, cookies, hosting logs, retention, your rights and how to contact us."
>
  <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
  <p class="lede">Version 2.1 &middot; Last updated: October 23, 2026 &middot; Applies to daoismhub.com and data.daoismhub.com</p>

  <h2>1. Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    DaoismHub ("the site", "we", "us") is an independent, editorially run educational
    reference about Chinese contemplative traditions, published at
    <strong>daoismhub.com</strong> and its structured-data companion
    <strong>data.daoismhub.com</strong>. The editor is <strong>Ding.W</strong>. For the
    purposes of data-protection law, the operator of DaoismHub is the controller of any
    personal data described in this policy.
  </p>
  <p>
    This policy explains, in plain language and in full, what is and is not processed when
    you visit either site, why, for how long, and what you can ask us to do about it. It is
    written to meet the transparency requirements of the EU General Data Protection
    Regulation (GDPR), the UK GDPR, and to describe our practices for visitors in other
    jurisdictions, including California (CCPA/CPRA).
  </p>

  <h2>2. The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No accounts, no comments, no newsletters, no contact forms.</strong> There is nothing to sign up for.</li>
    <li><strong>No analytics, no advertising, no tracking pixels, no social plugins.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>No cookies are set by us</strong> — neither first-party nor third-party.</li>
    <li>The only personal data we ever handle are <strong>(a)</strong> technical request data processed by our hosting provider in standard server logs, and <strong>(b)</strong> the contents of an email if you choose to write to us.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We do not sell, rent, trade or share personal data for money or for cross-context
    behavioural advertising. We have not done so in the past twelve months.
  </p>

  <h2>3. What we process, and why</h2>

  <h3>3.1 Technical data in hosting logs</h3>
  <p>
    Like virtually every website, the sites are delivered through hosting and
    content-delivery infrastructure (see section 5). When your browser requests a page,
    that infrastructure necessarily receives your IP address and standard request metadata
    (requested URL, time, user agent, referrer where sent) in order to route the response
    and protect the service against abuse. Those records are kept by the hosting provider
    for a short technical period in operational logs.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> delivering the pages you request, keeping the service available, and defending against malicious traffic.</li>
    <li><strong>Legal basis (GDPR Art. 6):</strong> our legitimate interest in operating and securing the site (Art. 6(1)(f)), and, where applicable, the performance of the service you requested (Art. 6(1)(b)).</li>
    <li><strong>Our access:</strong> we do not use these logs to identify individual visitors, build profiles, or measure individual behaviour.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3.2 Email correspondence</h3>
  <p>
    If you email us — for a correction, a sourcing question, a permission request, a
    privacy request or a collaboration enquiry — we receive your email address, whatever
    you write in the message, and any attachments you send.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> reading and answering your message, and keeping a record of corrections and permissions where that matters for editorial accuracy.</li>
    <li><strong>Legal basis (GDPR Art. 6):</strong> our legitimate interest in corresponding with readers and maintaining editorial accuracy (Art. 6(1)(f)); where your message asks us to do something for you, performance of that request (Art. 6(1)(b)); and consent (Art. 6(1)(a)) where we explicitly ask for it.</li>
    <li><strong>What we do not do:</strong> we do not add correspondents to a mailing list, do not use your address for marketing, and do not share it with third parties for their own purposes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3.3 What we do not process</h3>
  <p>We do not knowingly process any of the following:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>names, postal addresses or phone numbers collected through the sites (we do not ask for them);</li>
    <li>account credentials, passwords or profile data (there are no accounts);</li>
    <li>precise geolocation, device fingerprints or cross-site identifiers;</li>
    <li>behavioural, demographic or interest data;</li>
    <li>payment data (nothing is sold on the sites);</li>
    <li>special-category data as defined in GDPR Art. 9 — we ask that you do not send health or other sensitive details in an email unless it is necessary for your request.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Cookies and local storage</h2>
  <p>
    We set no cookies and use no local storage, session storage or similar client-side
    identifiers. Because no cookies are set, no cookie-consent banner is required or shown.
    The full position — including cookie categories and how to manage cookies in your
    browser — is set out in the dedicated <a href="/cookies/">Cookie Policy</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The search function on the Data Registry runs entirely in your browser: the query is
    matched against a static index file downloaded to your device and is never transmitted
    to us. There is no search history on our side.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your browser or our hosting provider may apply strictly necessary security measures
    (for example, a challenge cookie used to distinguish human visitors from automated
    attacks). Such measures, where they exist, are set by the infrastructure provider for
    security purposes and are not used by us to track you.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Hosting and service providers</h2>
  <p>
    The sites are static and are served through Cloudflare's content-delivery and hosting
    infrastructure. Cloudflare processes request data on our behalf as a processor in order
    to deliver content and protect the service. Cloudflare's own privacy terms apply to its
    processing; it does not receive data from us for any other purpose, because we have none
    to give.
  </p>
  <p>
    We use no other processors for visitor data. In particular, there is no analytics
    provider, no advertising network, no tag manager, no embedded video player, no comment
    system, no newsletter platform and no CRM connected to the sites.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Retention</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hosting logs:</strong> retained by the hosting provider for the short period set by its standard operational and security configuration, then discarded.</li>
    <li><strong>Email correspondence:</strong> kept only as long as needed to handle the matter, and afterwards only where we must keep a record — for example, a correction we made, a permission you granted, or a legal obligation. Routine correspondence is deleted when it is no longer needed.</li>
    <li><strong>Nothing else</strong> is stored, because nothing else is collected.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>7. Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Depending on where you live, you may have some or all of the following rights in
    relation to personal data we hold about you:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Access</strong> — to be told whether we process data about you and to receive a copy;</li>
    <li><strong>Rectification</strong> — to have inaccurate data corrected;</li>
    <li><strong>Erasure</strong> — to have data deleted where there is no overriding reason for us to keep it;</li>
    <li><strong>Restriction</strong> — to ask us to limit how we use it while a question is resolved;</li>
    <li><strong>Objection</strong> — to object to processing based on our legitimate interests;</li>
    <li><strong>Portability</strong> — to receive data you provided in a structured, machine-readable format;</li>
    <li><strong>Withdraw consent</strong> — where processing is based on consent, at any time, without affecting processing already carried out;</li>
    <li><strong>Non-discrimination</strong> — we will not treat you differently for exercising these rights.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because we hold almost no personal data, in most cases the practical answer will be that
    we hold nothing about you beyond an email you sent. We will still confirm that, in
    writing, within the time required by applicable law (normally one month under the GDPR).
    To make a request, email <strong>admin@daoismhub.com</strong> with the subject line
    "Privacy request". We may ask for enough information to locate any relevant
    correspondence; we will not ask for more than we need.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>California residents:</strong> you have the right to know what personal
    information is collected, used or disclosed; to request deletion; to correct inaccurate
    information; and to opt out of sale or sharing. We do not sell or share personal
    information, and we do not use it for cross-context behavioural advertising. You may
    exercise your rights using the same email address above. We will not discriminate
    against you for doing so.
  </p>

  <h2>8. International transfers</h2>
  <p>
    The sites are accessible worldwide and are delivered through a global content-delivery
    network, so a request may be served from infrastructure outside your country. Because we
    do not maintain a visitor database, there is no store of personal data to transfer
    between countries. Where email correspondence is concerned, messages are held in the
    email service we use to read and answer them; if you are in the EEA or the UK and your
    message is transferred outside that area, the transfer relies on the safeguards offered
    by that provider's terms and, where required, standard contractual clauses.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Security</h2>
  <p>
    The sites are static: there is no database of visitors, no login, and no server-side
    application that stores user input, which removes most of the usual attack surface.
    Traffic is served over HTTPS. Email is protected by the security measures of the email
    provider and by our own account security. No method of transmission or storage is
    perfectly secure, and we cannot guarantee absolute security — but we keep the amount of
    data at risk deliberately close to zero.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Children</h2>
  <p>
    The sites are general educational publications about history, texts and contemplative
    practice. They are not directed at children, and we do not knowingly collect personal
    data from anyone under 16. If you believe a child has sent us personal data by email,
    contact us and we will delete it.
  </p>

  <h2>11. Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control</h2>
  <p>
    We do not track visitors across sites, so there is no cross-site behaviour for a
    Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control signal to stop. We honour such signals in the
    sense that matters: no tracking is performed with or without them.
  </p>

  <h2>12. Links to other sites</h2>
  <p>
    Pages link to external resources — for example ctext.org for classical source texts, or
    scholarly publishers for cited research. Following such a link takes you to a site we do
    not control, and that site's privacy policy applies. We do not embed third-party
    content, widgets or trackers on our pages.
  </p>

  <h2>13. Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If we ever introduce a feature that changes this position — a newsletter, a contact
    form, analytics, embedded media — this policy will be updated <em>before</em> that
    feature goes live, the version number and date at the top will change, and the change
    will be described. The version published at this address is always the current one.
  </p>

  <h2>14. How to contact us, and how to complain</h2>
  <p>
    Privacy questions and requests: <strong>admin@daoismhub.com</strong> — please put
    "Privacy" in the subject line. We aim to acknowledge within seven days and to resolve
    within one month.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are in the EEA, the UK, Switzerland or another jurisdiction with a data-protection
    authority, you have the right to lodge a complaint with your local supervisory
    authority. We would appreciate the chance to address your concern first, but you do not
    need our permission to complain.
  </p>
  <p>
    Related pages: the <a href="/cookies/">Cookie Policy</a>, the
    <a href="/terms-of-service/">Terms of Service</a>, the
    <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> and the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
